reen screen work in the end  we still shot most of the film on  location and really it was the visual  effects department that was sort of  following us we didn't cater to them we  shot handheld they had to  - you know rotoscope to get the visual  effects  I think that's really Nolan's philosophy  is to have the visual effects trying to  play catch up with us and trying to work  off of our photography that's shot in  the real world the part where Alan page  opens up the mirrors a hundred percent  mechanical effect except that I was  removed from the shot yeah you know I  have the camera on my shoulder filming  the shot and standing right there in the  mirror and that's the only effect and in  that bit was I was erased from the shot  and and so that's that is an example of  sort of a Chris Nolan visual effect what  a clever director and in a smart visual  effects supervisor will do is they'll  consult with the director of photography  in their post work to make sure that the  work that they do gels with what the  cinematographer has been doing  throughout the film we do have our  little secrets we do have our little  paths that really nobody including the  director knows how we get there and so  the visual effects people need to be let  in on those secrets to be able to  recreate that and what they do no matter  what they're gonna have to match what  we've done so it's important for them to  pay close attention to what we're doing  so the process that I've had on the  Nolan pictures is when they're in the  process of visual effects months and  months and months after principal  photography the film the visual effects  supervisor generally sends me tips or  JPEGs of what they have going on the  visual effects I'll often take those  stills bring them into Photoshop do some  color work and some contrast work on  them to get them where I think it's  going to match the color timing and I'm  planning on doing on the film and and  then send it back to them and then they  work off of that so we go back and forth  with that process a little bit in post  my advice for cinematographers and for  young filmmakers is not to get hung up  in the technology and not to worry about  the changes in the technology think  about the craft of storytelling think of  the the craft and the art of  cinematography of  great composition of beautiful lighting  of appropriate camera movement that's  what filmmaking is about it's not about  bit you know bit depth and it's not  about megapixels and it's not even about  you know strips of film it is really  about the storytelling and putting it on  the screen in an appropriate way so  that's what they need to continue  focusing on the the next generation of  filmmakers what we're looking for are  people to give us interesting stories  not people who can drive machines  you
 

